<ContentPage title="Techno Park" subtitle="Open-source R&D lab and maker space.">
  <p>
    The Techno Park is BARK's innovation engine — a space where appropriate technology is researched, prototyped,
    documented, and shared openly. Everything built here is open-source by default. Below is real equipment data
    with verified price ranges for the machinery that will form the Techno Park's core capabilities.
  </p>

  <h2>Processing Equipment (Real Prices)</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Coffee Wet Mill</strong> — Depulper, fermentation tanks, washing channels, and drying beds.
      Small community-scale wet mill: <strong>$1,500&ndash;$12,000</strong> depending on capacity (500&ndash;5,000 kg/day).
      Essential for processing Yungas cherry to parchment coffee on-site rather than selling raw cherry at a fraction of the price.<sup>[1]</sup>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coffee Roaster</strong> — Production-grade drum roaster for specialty coffee.
      Range: <strong>$12,000&ndash;$40,000</strong> for 5&ndash;30 kg batch capacity.
      Converts green coffee ($4.60/lb) into roasted retail product ($25&ndash;45/lb).
      The single highest ROI piece of equipment in the Techno Park.<sup>[1]</sup>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cacao Bean-to-Bar Line</strong> — Roaster, winnower, grinder/conche, tempering machine, and molder.
      Small-batch (10&ndash;50 kg/day): <strong>$5,000&ndash;$25,000</strong>.
      Production-scale: <strong>$50,000&ndash;$100,000</strong>.
      Transforms raw cacao beans into finished chocolate bars retailing at $8&ndash;15 per 70g bar.<sup>[2]</sup>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Solar Dehydrators</strong> — For drying fruits, herbs, coffee, and cacao.
      DIY solar tunnel dryer: <strong>$50&ndash;$200</strong> in materials.
      Commercial stainless steel cabinet dryer: <strong>$900&ndash;$1,900</strong>.
      Critical for preserving Yungas tropical produce for export and long-term storage.<sup>[3]</sup>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Essential Oil Distillery</strong> — Steam distillation setup for extracting oils from aromatic plants.
      Copper or stainless steel still (20&ndash;100 liter): <strong>$2,000&ndash;$3,600</strong>.
      Yungas biodiversity provides raw materials; single-origin essential oils command $15&ndash;50 per 10ml at retail.<sup>[4]</sup>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fabrication & Making</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>3D Printing Workshop</strong> — FDM and resin printers for prototyping parts, tools, and replacement components.
      Capable setup: <strong>$2,000&ndash;$10,000</strong> for multiple printers covering different materials and resolutions.
      Critical for a remote community where ordering replacement parts means weeks of shipping. Print what you need.<sup>[5]</sup>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Open Source Ecology's GVCS</strong> — The <a href="https://www.opensourceecology.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Global Village Construction Set</a> is an open-source project developing 50 industrial machines needed to build a small civilization with modern comforts.
      Includes: compressed earth brick press, tractor, sawmill, 3D printer, CNC torch table, and more. All designs
      are freely available. Several machines are directly buildable in the Techno Park using local materials and fabrication.<sup>[6]</sup>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fab Lab Concept</strong> — Based on MIT's Fab Lab model, now with <strong>1,500+ labs in 100+ countries</strong> worldwide.
      A standard Fab Lab includes CNC router, laser cutter, 3D printer, electronics workbench, and vinyl cutter — total
      cost approximately $50,000&ndash;$100,000 for a full setup. The <a href="https://fab.city/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fab City</a> network provides design files,
      training curricula, and a global community of fabricators. BARK's Techno Park aims to be a Fab Lab node.<sup>[7]</sup>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Infrastructure Tech</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Solar arrays</strong> — 3.5&ndash;4.5 kWh/m&sup2;/day in the Yungas. Panels, inverters, and battery storage for community power independence.</li>
    <li><strong>Micro-hydro systems</strong> — Excellent potential given Yungas terrain and rainfall. 1&ndash;100 kW systems using the abundant streams and elevation drop. Complements solar during cloudy/rainy periods.</li>
    <li><strong>Water purification</strong> — Gravity-fed sand filtration, UV treatment, and ceramic filters. All maintainable with local materials and skills.</li>
    <li><strong>Mesh communications</strong> — LoRa mesh network for community-wide connectivity independent of cellular infrastructure. Combined with Starlink for internet backbone.</li>
    <li><strong>Agricultural automation</strong> — Soil moisture sensors, automated drip irrigation, greenhouse climate control. Low-cost IoT systems using ESP32 microcontrollers and open-source firmware.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">Total Techno Park Equipment Budget Estimate</p>
    <p>Core processing equipment (coffee, cacao, oils, dehydration) plus basic Fab Lab setup: approximately <strong>$80,000&ndash;$200,000</strong> depending on scale and whether equipment is built on-site from open-source designs or purchased commercially. This is a fraction of comparable facilities in developed countries.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How It Works</h2>
  <p>
    Resident makers and visiting researchers collaborate in shared workshop spaces. Every project is documented
    as open-source — plans, materials lists, build guides, test results. If BARK discovers a better way to purify
    water or generate power, that knowledge belongs to everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>DIY Ethos</h2>
  <p>
    We don't buy what we can build. We don't hoard what we can share. The Techno Park embodies the principle
    that technology sovereignty — the ability to understand, maintain, and improve your own systems — is a
    prerequisite for genuine autonomy. Open Source Ecology's GVCS, the Fab Lab network, and Earthship
    Biotecture's building techniques all prove this is practical, not idealistic.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">Makers, engineers, researchers</p>
    <p>If you build things and want them to matter, <a href="/en/participate">we want to hear from you</a>.</p>
  </div>

